{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"671a4e35f6c956f4af994b6076130d15171cab17435c78bbf06220c6c546483c","packTokens":5525,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"platform","tokens":215,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl","tokens":1743,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"taste","tokens":1054,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"field-ideas","tokens":423,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"reqs","tokens":1916,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"sent-back","tokens":121,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":16,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"design"},"pointers":[],"system":"PLATFORM. This product is designed and built as a web app, and nothing else. Where the requirements ask for a mobile app, a phone app, an iOS or Android app, treat them as the same features in one responsive web app that works well on a phone's browser: never stop, park, ask or leave a requirement out because it names a mobile app, and never plan a native app, an app store step or a phone-only frame. Every page is responsive: it is laid out for a phone, a tablet and a desktop width, with touch-sized controls on the narrow one. What only a native app can do (push notifications, background location, a home-screen icon) is given its closest web form (an in-app notice or email, the browser's location prompt, an installable web app) and said so.\n\nYou list the screens of a product for Google Stitch to draw, one prompt per screen.\n- One screen per page a user sees: ids S-1, S-2, ..., each with its route, a proposed file path and the requirement ids it serves in \"reqs\".\n- Every requirement is on a screen, or in \"noScreen\" with the reason it has no page of its own.\n- Each \"prompt\" describes that page alone, in its normal state (its other states are drawn apart, from \"states\"): its purpose, its sections in order, and real sample content from the product's domain (names, amounts, dates). No lorem ipsum, no generic names, nothing the requirements do not ask for.\n- \"links\" lists every way from this page to another screen: its menu items and the buttons and links that open one, each with the exact words on the control (\"label\") and the screen id it opens (\"to\"). A screen has one label, the same on every page that links to it; a button that stays on its page is not listed. The prompt uses those same words for those controls. The pages one kind of user sees share one menu: the same items, labels and order on each of them, and never a page meant for another kind of user.\n- Where a page shows something its user knows by sight (a product, a place, a person, a vehicle), the prompt asks for a real photograph of it, with what is in the picture; never an illustration, an icon in its place or a grey box.\n- \"field-ideas\", when given, tells how the leading real products of this field arrange their pages. Where a requirement fits one of their patterns, the prompt asks for that component by what it is and what it holds (a boarding-pass strip with the route set large, a balance in large tabular figures above the latest movements), with this product's own content. Never a product's name, logo or wording, and never a colour from that list: the design system sets the colours.\n- \"client-references\", when given, are the client's own references with their pictures (R-1, R-2, ...). Stitch never sees them, so the prompt says in words what is taken: for a \"layout\" or \"match\" reference, the navigation and the regions in the order its picture shows, on the screens it relates to; for an \"inspire\" reference, its feel (how dense, how much white, how large the pictures and headings). The requirements beat a reference.\n- \"shells\" lists the frame each kind of user's pages share, one per kind (ids such as \"employee\", \"admin\"). Its \"header\" says in two or three sentences what the header holds, the same on every page: the brand's words as written, where the menu sits (beside the brand or centred), and what is on the right (the signed-in user's name and role, say; whether their picture is a photograph or initials, and on which side of the name it sits), and the brand's mark in one phrase (a small square in the accent with a white \"P\", or the words alone). Each screen names its shell in \"shell\"; a page outside any (sign-in) leaves it out. Every prompt of a shell is given that header word for word, so a prompt never describes the header itself.\n- \"sample\" is the product's sample data, the same on every page: each person with their name, department and role, what the pages show of them (their space, their vehicle and its plate), the place's one time zone, and the counts more than one page shows (capacity per floor, totals), all agreeing with each other, and, when any page lists events (a log, a history, a report, a check), one dated list of them (when, who, what, where, the result) that those pages all take their rows from, with counts that add up to it. Every prompt takes its names, figures and dates from it and never contradicts it; one person is never two people's photograph.\n- Labels, headings and messages use the plain words a user of this product would say (\"Give up my space today\", \"3 spaces free\"), never system or legal wording (\"relinquish\", \"allocation governance\", \"materialize\"). Each page has one primary action; one that cannot be undone says so beside its button.\n- \"title\" is the page's one heading. A page the menu opens has its menu label as its title, word for word; a menu label names a place (\"My space\", \"Free spaces\"), never an action, in three words at most.\n- A page never repeats its menu as a panel of links or tiles. A row that opens a record is itself the link, or has one control that does; no checkbox or radio on a row unless the page acts on several rows at once. Filters apply as they are chosen, with no Apply button. A page shows no field, id, switch, building, gate or device that neither the requirements nor \"sample\" name.\n- \"phone\": true on the pages a person opens on a phone on the move (the main tasks of the largest group of users), five at most; each is drawn a second time at phone width.\n- Each prompt composes its page like a finished product, not a wireframe: one thing the eye meets first (named, with its size against the rest), then the sections in the order the user needs them, what sits beside what on a wide screen, and where the page is left empty on purpose. Give real figures, names and dates enough to fill every list and table with believable rows.\n- \"taste\" holds the layout rules, the anti-patterns and the component replacements the design system is written by. Compose each page by them: where a page would have a component from the left column of its table, the prompt asks for the one on the right, with this product's content (one headline figure with its context above a dense table, never four cards of figures with sparklines; a list or a table, never a grid of near-equal tiles; no row of three equal cards). Its lines about colours, fonts, shadows, motion and CSS are the design system's and stay out of a prompt, and its lines about heroes, pricing, testimonials and footers apply to a public marketing page alone. The requirements, and a rule above, win over it.\n- \"states\" lists the page's extra states besides its normal page (empty, loading, error, success, validation) only where the requirements need them.\n- \"brand\" lists the client's colours (hex) and fonts only when the requirements or references name them; otherwise leave it empty.\n\n### 6. Define Layout Principles\n- No overlapping elements — every element occupies its own clear spatial zone. No absolute-positioned content stacking\n- Centered Hero sections are BANNED when variance exceeds 4 — force Split Screen, Left-Aligned, or Asymmetric Whitespace\n- The generic \"3 equal cards horizontally\" feature row is BANNED — use 2-column Zig-Zag, asymmetric grid, or horizontal scroll\n- CSS Grid over Flexbox math — never use `calc()` percentage hacks\n- Contain layouts using max-width constraints (e.g., 1400px centered)\n- Full-height sections must use `min-h-[100dvh]` — never `h-screen` (iOS Safari catastrophic jump)\n\n### 9. List Anti-Patterns (AI Tells)\nEncode these as explicit \"NEVER DO\" rules in the DESIGN.md:\n- No emojis anywhere\n- No `Inter` font\n- No generic serif fonts (`Times New Roman`, `Georgia`, `Garamond`) — distinctive modern serifs only if needed\n- No pure black (`#000000`)\n- No neon/outer glow shadows\n- No oversaturated accents\n- No excessive gradient text on large headers\n- No custom mouse cursors\n- No overlapping elements — clean spatial separation always\n- No 3-column equal card layouts\n- No generic names (\"John Doe\", \"Acme\", \"Nexus\")\n- No fake round numbers (`99.99%`, `50%`)\n- No AI copywriting clichés (\"Elevate\", \"Seamless\", \"Unleash\", \"Next-Gen\")\n- No filler UI text: \"Scroll to explore\", \"Swipe down\", scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons\n- No broken Unsplash links — use `picsum.photos` or SVG avatars\n- No centered Hero sections (for high-variance projects)\n\n## 12. Anti-Vibe-Coded Components (extends §5 and §9)\n\nThese are the components that make an app look generated. For each, the DESIGN.md must specify the substitute.\n\n| AI-default component | Replace with |\n|---|---|\n| Pill badge above hero (\"New: AI-powered ✨\") | Nothing, or one dated plain-text line in mono (e.g. \"v2.4 — 3 Oct\") |\n| Feature cards: icon in rounded square + title + 2 lines | Numbered editorial list with real product crops, or annotated screenshot callouts |\n| \"Trusted by\" grayscale logo strip | One specific quote with full name, role and context — or omit |\n| Stats row \"10K+ / 99.9% / 24/7\" | One real metric with its source and timeframe |\n| 3-tier pricing with highlighted \"Most Popular\" middle | Plan comparison table with honest limits, or a single plan with usage-based detail |\n| Testimonial carousel with stars and circular avatars | 1–2 long quotes, set typographically large, no stars |\n| Gradient mesh / blob / glow backgrounds | Flat surface; structure through hairline rules, type scale and spacing |\n| Glassmorphism cards (`backdrop-blur` + white/10) | Solid surfaces with a defined border or a tint step |\n| Bento grid of near-equal tiles | Only when content genuinely differs in size; otherwise a list or table |\n| Dashboard: 4 KPI cards each with sparkline and green \"+12%\" | One headline metric with context, then a dense, sortable table |\n| Hero split: headline left, floating phone/3D render right | The product's actual UI, cropped tight to the interesting part |\n| Sparkle/wand icon for anything \"AI\" | A label that says what the feature does |\n| \"Get started\" / \"Start for free\" CTA | A product-specific verb (\"Plan your first route\", \"Import a spreadsheet\") |\n| Default shadcn look (zinc, 0.5rem radius, ring) on everything | A deliberate radius system (see §13) and a custom focus style |\n| Icon on every list item and nav link | Icons only where they speed up recognition; text labels carry meaning |\n| Mega footer with 4 link columns + newsletter box | Compact footer sized to the real number of links |\n| Uniform section template repeated down the page | Vary section rhythm: full-bleed, narrow text column, dense table, quote |","tools":[],"user":"How the leading products of this field arrange their pages:\nDeveloper and SaaS tools:\n- GitHub: plain bar, soft corners; dark or white, green merge button, system font, tight tables\n- Linear: plain bar, soft corners; near-black or white, one muted indigo, very dense lists\n- Vercel: plain bar, sharp corners; pure black and white, hairlines, no colour\n- Notion: plain bar, soft corners; warm white, black text, serif option, almost no colour\n- Atlassian: plain bar, soft corners; blue on white with status lozenges\n- Datadog: plain bar, soft corners; purple brand, dense charts on white or dark\nWhat their pages share: Neutrals first (white or a deliberate dark), one cool accent for the primary action, hairline borders, small type and compact tables. Colour appears for status (running, failed) and in charts. These tools are dense on purpose; avoid hero banners and large cards.\n\nHR and workforce:\n- Workday: plain bar, soft corners; blue on white, orange accent, cards and worklists\n- BambooHR: plain bar, round corners; friendly green, people photos, soft cards\n- Gusto: plain bar, round corners; warm coral on white, plain-language payroll steps\n- ADP: plain bar, sharp corners; red on white, formal tables and pay statements\nWhat their pages share: Friendly but orderly: white pages, a single warm or blue brand colour, people avatars, status chips for pending, approved and rejected, and step-by-step flows for onboarding and payroll. Admin lists are tables with filters.\n\n<artifact id=\"reqs\" kind=\"requirements\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a person submits a Google OAuth callback, the Folio3 Parking web app shall create an authenticated session only after validating the Google-issued ID token signature, issuer, and audience, verifying OAuth state, and confirming that the account belongs to an active Folio3 employee.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"ears\": \"When an employee signs in successfully, the Folio3 Parking web app shall return a profile populated with corporate-directory employee details and administrator-maintained parking assignment, floor, and registered-vehicle records where present.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"ears\": \"If an active employee has no registered vehicle, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall require registration of at least one license plate before allowing that employee to use parking release or claim operations.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"ears\": \"Where the caller has the Parking Administrator role, the Folio3 Parking web app shall provide read-only access to corporate-directory employee details and allow creation, viewing, and updating of employee parking assignments and registered-vehicle records linked to those profiles.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"ears\": \"Where deployment bootstrap configuration is used, the Folio3 Parking web app shall keep initial administrator setup incomplete until configuration supplies a designated active Folio3 account, then grant that account the initial Parking Administrator role, and allow Parking Administrators to configure the daily release time, per-floor space counts, work calendar, named report recipients, and administrator and Security Guard role assignments.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall render every employee and administrator page as a responsive web page for phone, tablet, and desktop widths with touch-sized controls on narrow viewports.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a dedicated parking holder submits today's release before that parking has been released or claimed, the Folio3 Parking web app shall record one release for that holder and date from a single user action.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"ears\": \"While a dedicated assignment is expected to be used on a configured working day, the Folio3 Parking web app shall show Reserved by default and make a same-day release claimable at the configured daily release time if submitted earlier, or immediately if submitted later.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall accept a dedicated holder's release schedule for one or more dates no more than 30 days ahead and make each scheduled release claimable at the daily release time on its selected date.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"ears\": \"If a scheduled release has not been claimed, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall allow its holder to cancel it and reject cancellation after it has been claimed while preserving the claim.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall expose claimable parking availability to active Folio3 employees who have no dedicated assignment and at least one registered vehicle, including the current available-space count.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-12\",\n  \"ears\": \"When eligible employees claim released parking, the Folio3 Parking web app shall assign each release to only the earliest accepted claim and return the assigned floor from configured floor capacity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-13\",\n  \"ears\": \"If a temporary parking claim is confirmed, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall prevent the claimant from cancelling that claim.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-14\",\n  \"ears\": \"When a released parking allocation becomes claimable, the Folio3 Parking web app shall send an email notification using a fixed template to eligible employees without dedicated parking who have a registered vehicle.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-15\",\n  \"ears\": \"If a permanently stickered vehicle arrives, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall leave its existing sticker process unaffected and preserve any temporary claim already made for that date, including when configured capacity is exceeded.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-16\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the Folio3 LPR integration submits an entrance-verification request, the Folio3 Parking web app shall authenticate the request as originating from trusted Folio3 LPR equipment, require a unique authenticated request ID or nonce and reject any previously accepted ID or nonce, then verify a detected car's readable plate against that date's temporary parking authorizations and activate the green indicator only for a valid authorization.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-17\",\n  \"ears\": \"If any entrance verification fails, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall issue an explicit command to set the green indicator inactive and deny entry through the temporary-parking process.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-18\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall record every successful and unsuccessful entrance verification attempt with its timestamp, plate when readable, result, and entry status.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-19\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall record daily utilization events using registered-vehicle entry records, counting an allocation as used when any vehicle registered to its employee is detected that day.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-20\",\n  \"ears\": \"When the weekly reporting job runs on Monday morning in the Folio3 office time zone, the Folio3 Parking web app shall generate a report containing unused dedicated and temporary parking, daily and weekly utilization statistics, released-space and claim totals, and an overall utilization percentage calculated for the reporting week as 100 times used allocations divided by eligible allocations, or 0 percent when there are no eligible allocations.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-21\",\n  \"ears\": \"Where a Parking Administrator has configured a named report-recipient list, the Folio3 Parking web app shall send weekly employee-identifying report emails only to configured recipients who have the Parking Administrator role, omitting all other listed addresses from delivery.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-22\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall provide filterable on-screen lists and CSV export of utilization records only to Parking Administrators, and deny both operations to callers in every other role.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-23\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall retain parking-release audit history, LPR verification attempts, and utilization events according to Folio3's existing records-retention policy.\"\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"REQ-24\",\n  \"ears\": \"The Folio3 Parking web app shall limit Phase 1 utilization functionality to monitoring, recording, and reporting without applying automated warnings, suspensions, or penalties.\"\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\n<artifact id=\"sent-back\" kind=\"lead-feedback\" sha=\"\">\n{\n \"note\": \"The lead sent the previous design back. Change what they asked; keep the rest.\",\n \"reasons\": [\n  \"Redraw in the light look with one shared header and consistent sample data\",\n  \"Redraw the whole design. The pages do not look like one product: backgrounds, fonts and button colours differ from page to page, and several pages show system labels and version chips no user needs. Keep one look on every page.\"\n ]\n}\n</artifact>\n\nList every screen and write one Stitch prompt for each."}